/** \page ros3_vfd Configuring the Read-Only S3 (ROS3) VFD
 *
 * Navigate back: \ref index "Main" / \ref UG / \ref AR_UG
 * <hr>
 *
 * \section sec_ros3_intro Introduction
 *
 * The Read-Only S3 virtual file driver, #H5FD_ROS3, reads HDF5 files from Amazon S3 and from
 * S3-compatible object stores such as MinIO and Ceph RADOS Gateway. It is selected with
 * #H5Pset_fapl_ros3.
 *
 * The driver takes its configuration from the same places as the AWS CLI and the AWS SDKs: the
 * File Access Property List (FAPL), AWS environment variables, and the shared AWS configuration
 * files in the <code>~/.aws</code> folder. It does not honor every AWS setting, so this page states
 * exactly what is and is not consulted.
 *
 * Settings are resolved in this order, with the first value found winning:
 *
 * -# The File Access Property List
 * -# Environment variables (HDF5-specific and AWS)
 * -# The shared AWS configuration file
 *
 * The profile read from the shared configuration file is the \c default profile, unless the
 * \c AWS_PROFILE environment variable names another one. The locations of the configuration files
 * themselves can be changed with the \c AWS_CONFIG_FILE and \c AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE
 * environment variables.
 *
 * \section sec_ros3_region AWS Region
 *
 * An AWS region is \Bold{required}, including when reading from an S3-compatible object store that
 * has no concept of AWS regions; in that case, any value the store accepts will do. The driver
 * looks for it in:
 *
 * -# The \c aws_region field of #H5FD_ros3_fapl_t, set with #H5Pset_fapl_ros3
 * -# The \c AWS_REGION environment variable
 * -# The \c AWS_DEFAULT_REGION environment variable
 * -# The \c region setting of the active profile in <code>~/.aws/config</code>
 *
 * If no region can be determined, opening a file fails. No default region is assumed. This is
 * deliberate, since guessing a region can cause unintended cross-region data transfer charges.
 *
 * \section sec_ros3_credentials Credentials
 *
 * If the \c authenticate field of #H5FD_ros3_fapl_t is true, credentials are taken \Bold{only}
 * from the FAPL: \c secret_id and \c secret_key must both be non-empty, and a session token, if
 * needed, must be supplied separately with #H5Pset_fapl_ros3_token. Nothing else is consulted in
 * this case.
 *
 * Otherwise, credentials are resolved by the default credential provider chain of the
 * <a href="https://github.com/awslabs/aws-c-auth">aws-c-auth</a> library, the same chain used by
 * the AWS CLI and the AWS SDKs:
 *
 * -# The \c AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, \c AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, and \c AWS_SESSION_TOKEN environment
 *    variables
 * -# The active profile in the shared configuration files, which covers assuming a role
 *    (\c role_arn with \c source_profile or \c credential_source), an external credential helper
 *    program (\c credential_process), and static credentials (\c aws_access_key_id,
 *    \c aws_secret_access_key, \c aws_session_token)
 * -# STS \c AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity, using \c AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE, \c AWS_ROLE_ARN and
 *    \c AWS_ROLE_SESSION_NAME, or the \c web_identity_token_file profile setting, as used by IAM
 *    Roles for Service Accounts on EKS
 * -# ECS and EKS container credentials, when \c AWS_CONTAINER_CREDENTIALS_RELATIVE_URI or
 *    \c AWS_CONTAINER_CREDENTIALS_FULL_URI is set
 * -# EC2 instance metadata
 *
 * If none of these yield credentials, the driver falls back to anonymous credentials and sends
 * unsigned requests. This is how objects in public buckets are read. It also means that a
 * misconfigured profile produces unsigned requests rather than an error, which usually surfaces
 * later as an HTTP 403 response from the server.
 *
 * \note AWS IAM Identity Center (SSO) is not supported directly. A profile that relies on
 *       \c sso_session or \c sso_start_url alone is not part of the chain above and will fall back
 *       to anonymous credentials. SSO credentials can still be used by way of the
 *       \c credential_process setting.
 *
 * \note Because EC2 instance metadata is attempted by default, opening a file on a machine that is
 *       not an EC2 instance may be delayed while that step times out. Set the
 *       \c AWS_EC2_METADATA_DISABLED environment variable to \c true to skip it.
 *
 * \section sec_ros3_endpoint Endpoint URL
 *
 * An alternate endpoint URL is needed to read from an S3-compatible object store. It is looked for
 * in:
 *
 * -# The File Access Property List, set with #H5Pset_fapl_ros3_endpoint
 * -# The \c AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3 environment variable
 * -# The \c AWS_ENDPOINT_URL environment variable
 * -# The \c endpoint_url entry under the \c s3 key of the <code>[services ...]</code> section
 *    named by the active profile's \c services entry
 * -# The \c endpoint_url entry of the active profile itself
 *
 * The last two are read from the shared AWS configuration file, and follow the endpoint
 * precedence documented by AWS. Either of these forms works:
 *
 * \code{.ini}
 * # the "global endpoint" form
 * [default]
 * endpoint_url = https://minio.example.org:9000
 *
 * # the service-specific form, which takes precedence over the above
 * [profile dev]
 * services = my-services
 *
 * [services my-services]
 * s3 =
 *   endpoint_url = https://minio.example.org:9000
 * \endcode
 *
 * \note Every source other than the File Access Property List can be suppressed by setting the
 *       \c AWS_IGNORE_CONFIGURED_ENDPOINT_URLS environment variable, or the
 *       \c ignore_configured_endpoint_urls entry of the active profile, to \c true.
 *
 * \section sec_ros3_addressing Request Addressing Style
 *
 * The driver uses virtual-hosted-style requests (<code>https://bucket.host/key</code>) by default
 * where it can, and path-style requests (<code>https://host/bucket/key</code>) otherwise. It uses
 * path-style requests when:
 *
 * \li the #HDF5_ROS3_VFD_FORCE_PATH_STYLE environment variable is set to anything other than
 *     \c false, \c off, or \c 0; or
 * \li an alternate endpoint URL is in effect; or
 * \li the bucket name contains a '.'
 *
 * The \c addressing_style setting of the shared AWS configuration file is not consulted.
 *
 * \section sec_ros3_debug Debugging Output
 *
 * Three environment variables control diagnostic output:
 *
 * <table>
 * <tr><th>Variable<th>Effect
 * <tr><td>#HDF5_ROS3_VFD_DEBUG
 *     <td>Prints the driver's own debugging information to \c stderr
 * <tr><td>#HDF5_ROS3_VFD_LOG_LEVEL
 *     <td>Enables aws-c-s3 library logging at \c error, \c info, \c debug or \c trace level
 * <tr><td>#HDF5_ROS3_VFD_LOG_FILE
 *     <td>Destination for that logging: \c stdout, \c stderr, or a filename. Defaults to
 *         #H5FD_ROS3_VFD_DEFAULT_LOG_FILE
 * </table>
 *
 * Setting \c HDF5_ROS3_VFD_LOG_LEVEL to \c trace is the most direct way to see which credential
 * provider answered and which endpoint and request style were used.
 *
 * \section sec_ros3_build Building
 *
 * The ROS3 VFD requires the <a href="https://github.com/awslabs/aws-c-s3">aws-c-s3</a> library and
 * is enabled with the CMake option \c HDF5_ENABLE_ROS3_VFD. See \c docs/INSTALL_S3.md in the HDF5
 * source distribution for instructions on obtaining aws-c-s3 and on running the ROS3 test suite.
 *
 * <hr>
 * Navigate back: \ref index "Main" / \ref UG / \ref AR_UG
 *
 */
